@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@inject PvSystemInfo Site

@*
    The sign-out control is a plain HTML form posting to a non-Blazor endpoint (Program.cs maps
    POST /logout), not a Blazor event handler -- this layout wraps pages that may or may not be
    interactive (Login isn't), and a form submission works identically either way.
*@

<header class="topbar">
    <span class="brand">
        <SunMark />
        Gleanvolt
    </span>
    @*
        Which installation this is (issue #111). The product name says what the software is; on a
        second tab onto a second roof, that is the one thing you already know. Shown to anyone who can
        reach the port, including on the sign-in page: it is a name, and the address and coordinates
        stay behind the authorised Settings page.
    *@
    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Site.Name))
    {
        <span class="site">@Site.Name</span>
    }
    @*
        On a phone the links don't fit beside the brand, so below the breakpoint in site.css they
        fold behind a Menu button. The toggle is a checkbox rather than a Blazor handler for the same
        reason the sign-out control is a form: this layout also wraps the static sign-in page, and a
        checkbox opens the menu whether or not a circuit is live. menu.js closes it again after a link
        is followed, since enhanced navigation keeps the header -- and the box's checked state.

        The links, and the button that folds them, only for the audience they are for: on the default
        policy, like the feed alert below, so with sign-in required the sign-in page shows the name
        and nothing else -- every link would only have bounced back to it -- and with sign-in off
        nothing changes.
    *@
    <AuthorizeView Context="viewer">
        <Authorized>
            <input type="checkbox" id="menu-toggle" class="menu-toggle" aria-label="Menu" />
            <label for="menu-toggle" class="menu-button" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="menu-icon"></span>Menu
            </label>
            <div class="menu">
                <nav>
                    <NavLink href="/" Match="NavLinkMatch.All">Overview</NavLink>
                    <NavLink href="/charging-plan">Charging plan</NavLink>
                    <NavLink href="/sessions">Sessions</NavLink>
                    <NavLink href="/energy">Energy</NavLink>
                    <NavLink href="/forecast">Forecast</NavLink>
                    <NavLink href="/car">Car</NavLink>
                    @* The installation and the controller's health, behind one link (#255): the two pages
                       an owner opens least, and the reason seven links fit beside the name. *@
                    <NavLink href="/settings">Settings</NavLink>
                </nav>
                @* A named policy rather than AuthorizeView's default: "is somebody actually signed in" has to
                   stay true regardless of Web:RequireAuthentication, or this control would show for an
                   anonymous visitor once RequireAuthentication is off and DefaultPolicy allows everyone. *@
                <AuthorizeView Policy="Authenticated">
                    <Authorized>
                        <form method="post" action="/logout" class="signout">
                            <button type="submit">Sign out</button>
                        </form>
                    </Authorized>
                </AuthorizeView>
            </div>
        </Authorized>
    </AuthorizeView>
</header>

<main class="content">
    @*
        A feed that has stopped and needs a person (#140) is worth saying on every page, not only on
        the dashboard card it belongs to: it does not clear itself, and a car that has quietly stopped
        reporting looks exactly like a car that is parked.

        Inside AuthorizeView on the default policy -- which is the audience of every page except the
        sign-in one, whether or not Web:RequireAuthentication is on. The sign-in page keeps saying only
        what it said before: the product, and the installation's name.
    *@
    <AuthorizeView>
        <Authorized>
            <VehicleFeedAlert />
        </Authorized>
    </AuthorizeView>

    @Body
</main>
